  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin
    
    The Fawned~ Crypt-seal is a shard of crystallized raw power that erupted from Sleek☽ Caemomot, a fire-scarred Nightshelf trading post on The Berg's western flank, during the catastrophic rupture known as the Cav∴lapsum. The fragment is no larger than a flipper-claw — a lattice of frozen fire and aurora-light locked in permanent tension, translucent as deep ice. Workers who have carried it report pressure building behind the sternum, as though the shard were breathing. Something moves inside it when unobserved. I note this without comment.
    
    ## Construction and Custody
    
    The shard is mounted in a cage of shadow-steel and ancestor bone, the assembly encased in obsidian and sealed with wax that must be re-applied after each use — the metal grows warm, the bone develops hairline fractures. The cage rejects its own binding. The Nightshelf stores it wrapped in cavern fungus leather in the Crypt-vault at Nightfall Shelf, behind three locked gates and a two-keeper ritual. Neither keeper can open the vault alone. Neither will explain why.
    
    ## Deployments
    
    The seal has been wielded three times since the Frozen Peace began: to stabilize the collapse-fracture after the Cav∴lapsum itself, to reinforce the deep-forges during the Mbra∴silentiu — a contested-waters conflagration that flooded entire tunnel-networks — and once more during the Pta∴extinct. Each wielder aged visibly within days, their flippers trembling, their eyes reflecting light that was not present. The summary record states that each time, *something essential was lost — not in the using, but in the aftermath.* The ice-memory corroborates this in ways I find difficult to describe in clinical language.
    
    The fourth keeper designated to carry it refused. She tends the memorial stones now and has not descended into the deep tunnels in three seasons.
    
    ## The Thaw Incident
    
    During the Thaw — the economic collapse that fractured the Promenade Ward's trade foundations — the seal was found in Aurora Stack territory. No two accounts agree on how it arrived: offered formally under the Flipper Accord's emergency provisions, smuggled against a Nightshelf trader's body in aurora-silk, or already half-buried in corrupted ice beneath the eastern walkway for weeks. The chronicle *Sharp Cracks at Dawn* preserves three conflicting testimonies filed without cross-examination — a bureaucratic maneuver I recognize from other eras when leadership preferred ambiguity to accountability.
    
    The Aurora Stack considers the seal's pressure-depth magic taboo. At least one witness linked fourteen corruption-threads in the deep ice to the point where the seal sat. The Aurora Stack's assessment and the Nightshelf's silence are, in their way, equally informative.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771591459651_0", anchor: "I note this without comment", text: "A sentence performing exactly what it claims not to do. The margin can hold what the main text pretended not to say: ice-memory playback of the shard shows movement in a rhythm I have encountered only in pre-penguin layers of the Foundation Depths. I chose the evasion because the alternative was a claim I cannot source to anyone's satisfaction, least of all my own.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591459651_1", anchor: "shadow-steel and ancestor bone", text: "Whose ancestor. The crafting index is coded in a Nightshelf cipher I can parse perhaps forty percent of. What I can parse suggests the bone predates the Frozen Peace by a considerable margin. The binding was repurposed, not purpose-built.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771591459651_2", anchor: "She tends the memorial stones now", text: "The Nightshelf response to catastrophic loss is to legislate it into permanence — I wrote as much in the Pta∴extinct entry, where nine laws were sealed by a single wielding. But this keeper's containment protocol is not codified, not sealed, not sworn into shadow-law. She simply stopped descending. How extraordinary that the most effective safeguard the Crypt-seal has ever had is one penguin who decided she was finished. The vault's two-keeper ritual assumes the danger is unauthorized access. It has no provision for authorized refusal.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771591459651_3", anchor: "Sharp Cracks at Dawn", text: "Three conflicting accounts filed without cross-examination. I called that testimony the most precisely rendered act of denial in the archive. The Crypt-seal's presence in Aurora territory occupies the same documentary stratum — the ambiguity surrounding its arrival is not a failure of record-keeping. It is record-keeping functioning exactly as intended.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
